% ============================================================================
% food-group-alternative-form — for the food groups whose OWN definitional
% sentence also names a non-solid-food form that still counts, that
% alternative form (adj-facts-stdlib, NUTRITION).
% ============================================================================
% A sibling to the already-shipped `food-groups.adj` (`food_group(food,
% food_group)`, which sorts whole, solid foods into one of the five MyPlate
% groups). Three of that table's own per-group definitional sentences,
% already quoted verbatim in its header, ALSO name a non-solid-food
% alternative that counts toward the SAME group — a fact the solid-food-
% only schema had no room for:
%
%   fruits → hundred_percent_fruit_juice
%     "The Fruit Group includes all fruits and 100% fruit juice."
%
%   vegetables → hundred_percent_vegetable_juice
%     "Any vegetable or 100% vegetable juice counts as part of the
%      Vegetable Group."
%
%   dairy → lactose_free_milk, fortified_soy_milk, fortified_soy_yogurt
%     "The Dairy Group includes milk, yogurt, cheese, lactose-free milk and
%      fortified soy milk and yogurt."
%
% Recall is a binding query:
%
%     ? food_group_alternative_form(dairy, $Form)
%       % lactose_free_milk ; fortified_soy_milk ; fortified_soy_yogurt
%
% This is a native `table` (ADJ-TABLES RS-5): each `row` lowers to a ground
% relation `food_group_alternative_form(food_group, alternative_form)`
% carrying the citation, so the lookup above is the ordinary SLD binding
% query — the engine returns the alternative form WITH its source, on the
% CPU, with zero model calls, and abstains on grains and protein, since
% NEITHER of those two groups' own cited definitional sentences names a
% comparable alternative form — the same honest-abstention-by-simple-
% absence discipline `coin-penny-discontinued.adj` uses for every coin but
% the penny. The query also runs BACKWARD — bind an alternative form and
% recall which group it belongs to:
%
%     ? food_group_alternative_form($Group, hundred_percent_fruit_juice)
%       % fruits
%
% Provenance: reproduces, byte-for-byte, the SAME USDA MyPlate per-group
% spans already quoted inside `food-groups.adj`'s own header — no new
% WebFetch, only a different schema decoding the alternative-form half of
% those already-verified quotes. `trust authoritative` — the same tier
% `food-groups.adj` already carries (USDA MyPlate, a primary U.S.
% government source).
% (See ../README.md; feedback_nothing_human_authored,
% feedback_adj_only_shipped_artifacts.)
% ============================================================================

table food_group_alternative_form {
    columns food_group, alternative_form

    row (fruits, hundred_percent_fruit_juice)         % MyPlate: "The Fruit Group includes all fruits and 100% fruit juice."
    row (vegetables, hundred_percent_vegetable_juice)  % MyPlate: "Any vegetable or 100% vegetable juice counts as part of the Vegetable Group."
    row (dairy, lactose_free_milk)                     % MyPlate: "The Dairy Group includes milk, yogurt, cheese, lactose-free milk and fortified soy milk and yogurt."
    row (dairy, fortified_soy_milk)                    % MyPlate: "The Dairy Group includes milk, yogurt, cheese, lactose-free milk and fortified soy milk and yogurt."
    row (dairy, fortified_soy_yogurt)                  % MyPlate: "The Dairy Group includes milk, yogurt, cheese, lactose-free milk and fortified soy milk and yogurt."

    source "The Fruit Group includes all fruits and 100% fruit juice."
    locator "https://www.myplate.gov/eat-healthy/fruits"
    trust authoritative

    cites "Any vegetable or 100% vegetable juice counts as part of the Vegetable Group." locator "https://www.myplate.gov/eat-healthy/vegetables"
    cites "The Dairy Group includes milk, yogurt, cheese, lactose-free milk and fortified soy milk and yogurt." locator "https://www.myplate.gov/eat-healthy/dairy"
}
